  <div class="primer-page">
    <section class="hero primer-hero"><div><span class="eyebrow">A HANDS-ON INTRODUCTION</span><h1>Control logic, made visible.</h1><p>CDL describes what a building controller computes. Typed signals enter blocks, connections carry values, and outputs command equipment. Follow the example, change a parameter, and run the building to see the consequence.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="solid-button" href="#lesson-blocks">Start with blocks ↓</a><a class="outline-button" href="#glossary">Block glossary ↗</a></div></div><div class="hero-graphic"><div class="mini-node">Zone air temp <strong>{{ measured.toFixed(1) }}°C</strong></div><span class="flow-arrow">→</span><div class="mini-node lime">Error × gain <strong>{{ gain.toFixed(1) }}×</strong></div><span class="flow-arrow">→</span><div class="mini-node">Demand <strong>{{ demand.toFixed(1) }}</strong></div></div></section>
    <nav class="lesson-nav" aria-label="Primer lessons"><a href="#lesson-blocks">01 · Blocks</a><a href="#lesson-connections">02 · Connections</a><a href="#lesson-state">03 · Time & state</a><a href="#lesson-building">04 · Building loop</a><a href="#lesson-points">05 · Brick points</a><a href="#glossary">Glossary ↗</a></nav>

    <section id="lesson-blocks" class="lesson"><div class="lesson-copy"><span class="eyebrow">LESSON 01 / BLOCKS</span><h2>Small functions make a sequence</h2><p>A block has typed inputs and outputs, plus settings called parameters. The bundled VAV controller combines Constants, Subtract, Greater, a limited PI controller, Switches, and Limiters to turn zone temperature into damper and reheat commands.</p><div class="lesson-note"><strong>Try it</strong><span>Change the temperature or gain. This small preview updates immediately; the full VAV sequence is in the editor.</span></div></div><div class="lesson-demo"><div class="demo-header"><span>LIVE CALCULATION</span><span class="status-dot">● Evaluating</span></div><div class="field-row"><label class="field"><span>Zone air temperature · °C</span><input v-model.number="measured" type="range" min="16" max="30" step="0.1"/><b>{{ measured.toFixed(1) }}°C</b></label><label class="field"><span>Gain · k</span><input v-model.number="gain" type="range" min="0" max="3" step="0.1"/><b>{{ gain.toFixed(1) }}</b></label></div><div class="equation-view"><code>y = k × (zone_temp − 22 °C)</code><strong>{{ demand.toFixed(2) }}</strong></div></div></section>

    <section id="lesson-connections" class="lesson"><div class="lesson-copy"><span class="eyebrow">LESSON 02 / CONNECTIONS</span><h2>Signals follow the wires</h2><p>Connections link a block output to a compatible input. The graph uses Real and Boolean values throughout the bundled building; the editor also accepts Integer and String connectors for imported or custom graphs. A composite block exposes a small boundary around many internal blocks.</p><div class="lesson-note"><strong>Watch for</strong><span>A missing input, two drivers on one input, a type mismatch, or a connection cycle.</span></div></div><div class="lesson-demo"><div class="demo-header">VAV CONTROL PATH</div><div class="signal-path"><div><small>01 · INPUT</small><strong>Zone air temperature</strong><span>Real · °C</span></div><i>→</i><div><small>02 · BLOCK</small><strong>Limited PI</strong><span>Cooling setpoint + measured</span></div><i>→</i><div><small>03 · OUTPUT</small><strong>Damper position command</strong><span>Real · 0–1</span></div></div><p class="muted small">Open a VAV composite in the editor to see the full path, including mode checks and the damper limit.</p></div></section>

    <section id="lesson-state" class="lesson"><div class="lesson-copy"><span class="eyebrow">LESSON 03 / TIME & STATE</span><h2>Some blocks remember earlier steps</h2><p>Constants and gains react immediately. A filter, delay, hysteresis, or PI controller stores state across steps. The runtime evaluates the graph in dependency order every 15 minutes, then advances the building thermal model.</p><div class="lesson-note"><strong>Try it</strong><span>Move the input. A shorter filter time constant follows a change faster.</span></div></div><div class="lesson-demo"><div class="demo-header">FIRST-ORDER FILTER PREVIEW</div><label class="field"><span>New input</span><input v-model.number="filterInput" type="range" min="0" max="100" step="1"/><b>{{ filterInput }}</b></label><label class="field"><span>Time constant · minutes</span><input v-model.number="filterTime" type="range" min="1" max="60" step="1"/><b>{{ filterTime }} min</b></label><div class="filter-bars"><div><span>Input</span><i :style="{width:filterInput+'%'}"/></div><div><span>After 15 minutes</span><i :style="{width:filterOutput+'%'}"/></div></div><p class="muted small">This preview assumes the filter previously held zero. A new runtime filter initializes from its first input.</p></div></section>

    <section id="lesson-building" class="lesson"><div class="lesson-copy"><span class="eyebrow">LESSON 04 / CLOSED LOOP</span><h2>The building responds back</h2><p>Two AHUs each serve five VAV zones. Their fans limit actual airflow. Each AHU mixes 20% outdoor air with return air; its coil heats or cools toward the commanded supply temperature as plant output allows. VAV dampers and hot-water reheat then affect zone temperature. At the next step, controllers receive the new zone and supply temperatures.</p><p>The heat pump serves a selected share of heating and cooling, with boiler and chiller covering the remainder. The model charges energy for heat and cooling actually used. VAV heating and cooling demand outputs are diagnostic; the plant request comes from calculated coil and reheat loads.</p><div class="lesson-note"><strong>Explore it</strong><span>Edit an AHU fan gain or VAV setpoint, run the synthetic 2025 year, and compare comfort and energy with the baseline.</span></div></div><div class="lesson-demo building-stack"><div class="plant-row"><div>CHILLER</div><div>BOILER</div><div>HEAT PUMP</div></div><div class="pipe-row"><span>CHILLED WATER LOOP</span><span>HOT WATER LOOP</span></div><div class="plant-row"><div>AHU 1 <small>Zones 1–5</small></div><div>AHU 2 <small>Zones 6–10</small></div></div><div class="zone-dots"><i v-for="n in 10" :key="n" :title="`Zone ${n}`"/></div><p class="muted small">Airflow and duct pressure are normalized. Equipment capacities and dynamics are simplified.</p></div></section>

    <section id="lesson-points" class="lesson"><div class="lesson-copy"><span class="eyebrow">LESSON 05 / POINT MEANING</span><h2>Brick gives points a shared meaning</h2><p>Brick classifies a measured zone temperature as <code>brick:Zone_Air_Temperature_Sensor</code>, an AHU target as <code>brick:Supply_Air_Temperature_Setpoint</code>, and a VAV output as <code>brick:Damper_Position_Command</code>. The example attaches those classes and readable labels to equipment-facing ports while keeping their connector IDs stable for saved graphs.</p><div class="lesson-note"><strong>Look it up</strong><span>The <a href="#glossary">glossary</a> defines every supported block type. Block tooltips show the point class where one applies.</span></div></div><div class="lesson-demo"><div class="demo-header">POINT ROLES</div><div class="signal-path"><div><small>MEASURED</small><strong>Zone air temperature</strong><span>brick:Sensor · °C</span></div><i>→</i><div><small>CALCULATED</small><strong>Controller logic</strong><span>CDL blocks</span></div><i>→</i><div><small>COMMANDED</small><strong>Damper position</strong><span>brick:Command · 0–1</span></div></div><p class="muted small">A calculation or normalized model value is not presented as a physical sensor.</p></div></section>
  </div>
</template>

<script setup lang="ts">
import { computed, ref } from 'vue'
const measured=ref(24)
const gain=ref(1.2)
const demand=computed(()=>gain.value*(measured.value-22))
const filterInput=ref(72)
const filterTime=ref(20)
const filterOutput=computed(()=>filterInput.value*(1-Math.exp(-15/filterTime.value)))
</script>
